Building selscan from source
============================

selscan needs a C++17 compiler and zlib. It has been built with GCC 11
(Rocky Linux 9) and Apple clang 16. It no longer uses GSL. Normalisation is run as `selscan norm`; there
is no separate norm binary.

    cd src
    make            # builds src/selscan
    make check      # optional: runs the regression suite in tests/
    make install    # optional: copies selscan to /usr/local/bin
                    # (make install PREFIX=$HOME installs to ~/bin)

The binary is written to src/ (src/selscan.exe on Windows). Object files
go to src/build/; `make clean` removes them and the binary.

Platforms
---------

Linux: install a compiler and the zlib headers, e.g.
`dnf install gcc-c++ zlib-devel` (RHEL, Rocky, Alma, Fedora) or
`apt install g++ zlib1g-dev` (Debian, Ubuntu).

macOS: install the Xcode command line tools (`xcode-select --install`).
zlib is part of macOS.

Windows: use MSYS2 (https://www.msys2.org). In the MINGW64 shell, run
`pacman -S make mingw-w64-x86_64-gcc mingw-w64-x86_64-zlib`, then build as
above.

Options
-------

Set these on the make command line, e.g. `make STATIC=1`:

    CXX=...         compiler to use
    CXXFLAGS=...    optimisation and debugging flags (default -O3)
    STATIC=1        Linux: link libstdc++, libgcc and zlib statically, so the
                    binary needs only the system glibc. Windows: a fully
                    static .exe.
    UNIVERSAL=1     macOS: one binary for Apple Silicon and Intel Macs.
    MACOS_MIN=11.0  macOS: the oldest macOS the binary should run on.

A debug build with the undefined-behaviour sanitizer:

    make CXXFLAGS="-O1 -g -fsanitize=undefined" LDFLAGS=-fsanitize=undefined
